From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 11/15] dndcp: Start a native Wayland drag from a layer shell
 overlay

A host to guest drag is an XDND drag from the detection window, a Gtk
window on the x11 backend, because GDK is pinned to that backend process
wide. Under a Wayland session the window is an Xwayland client, and
whether its drag reaches a native Wayland application depends on the
compositor. KWin and Mutter bridge an X11 drag to Wayland clients, and
wlroots does not. That is wlroots issue #841. On sway every drop onto a
Wayland application was lost, six times out of six. The source logged
drag-begin and the target saw nothing, while the same drag onto an
Xwayland target succeeded. A plain Gtk drag source on the x11 backend
behaves the same with open-vm-tools absent, so the limit is in the
compositor.

A wl_data_device drag is core protocol, and reaches native clients on
wlroots, KWin and Mutter alike. A client can start one only from the
serial of a button press on a surface of its own, and it is not told
where the pointer is. The new waylandDrag/ module provides those
surfaces and starts the drag from the press. It keeps its own connection
to WAYLAND_DISPLAY, as waylandClipboard/ does, and binds
wl_data_device_manager and zwlr_layer_shell_v1. WaylandDrag_Arm() copies
the formats to offer and maps a transparent layer shell overlay over
each output, so that a press anywhere lands on one of them. An output
that goes away is dropped with its overlay. Where layer shell is absent,
as on GNOME, WaylandDrag_Init() fails, and it does not ask such a
compositor again.

PointerButton() starts the drag from inside the handler of the press,
with its serial, because compositors refuse a wl_pointer.enter serial.
The source offers the armed formats with the copy action. There is no
drag icon, since the host draws one on its side of the window.
WaylandDrag_StartOnPress() reads the module's own connection until the
press arrives, for at most the caller's timeout.

The overlay must leave the drag once the grab exists. sway and KWin
honour an empty input region during a drag, but Mutter ignores it, so
the overlays are unmapped instead, which works on all three. Committing
a null buffer returns a layer surface to its initial state, so the
configure that follows must not attach the buffer again.

The module has to find out whether start_drag succeeded. A drag across
surfaces without a data device produces no events for as long as it
lasts, so a timeout on events would end a real drag. A wl_display.sync
follows start_drag instead. The compositor sends cancelled for a refused
drag before it answers the sync, so an answer while the source still
exists means the grab exists, and a watchdog covers a compositor that
answers nothing. The drag ends on cancelled or dnd_finished, and a
target that never sends dnd_finished after a drop is given 10 s. The
caller's done() runs exactly once, although some compositors send both
events.

Offer only the formats that have bytes. A wl_data_source.send can only
end in a closed pipe, so serving nothing for an offered type reads at
the other end as a successful transfer of zero bytes. GTK turns an empty
text/uri-list into a NULL vector rather than an empty one, and libfm
walks that vector without checking it, so PCManFM crashes on an offer
with nothing behind it. WaylandDrag_Arm() drops such formats and fails
if none remain, which leaves the drag to XDND.

Waits for the compositor stop at a deadline and never iterate the
caller's main loop, which also carries the RPC channel. Iterating it
would dispatch RPC commands from inside an RPC handler. A send writes
the requested type to a non-blocking pipe and stops at a deadline, and
all the sends of one drag share a budget, so a target that stops reading
cannot stall the main loop. The module's own data device events show
when the overlay is offered the drag, which would mean the overlay is
taking the drop from the application under it, and it destroys the
offers they carry. WaylandDrag_Pump() handles what the compositor has
sent without waiting, and WaylandDrag_Cancel() ends a drag at once by
destroying the source.

wlr-layer-shell is not part of wayland-protocols, so its XML from
wlr-protocols is added next to its user, and the protocol code is
generated from it when the plugin is built. The XML refers to xdg_popup,
so the xdg-shell interface code is generated too. The next patch uses
the module.
